qtgrass (0.3.6) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix raster stripe artifact root cause: the lat/lon safety clamp block
    unconditionally overwrote vpRegion.ns_res with viewport resolution,
    undoing the native-resolution fix for geographic (lat/lon) locations.
    Result: renderH == dstH, nearest-neighbour branch always taken → stripes.
  * Fix: remove ns_res override from lat/lon clamp block; clamp only
    north/south to ±90°, let G_adjust_Cell_head recompute rows from useNsRes.
  * Change oversample strategy: use OVERSAMPLE=4 (4× dstH rows, capped at
    native resolution) instead of reading ALL native rows.  This avoids
    reading tens of thousands of rows for highly zoomed-out views while still
    eliminating stripe artefacts via area averaging.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Thu, 21 May 2026 09:00:00 +0000

qtgrass (0.3.5) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix horizontal-stripe artifact in raster rendering (aspect, slope, …):
    root cause: GRASS Rast_get_row() uses nearest-neighbour row mapping
    (compute_window_row → r = (int)(row*C1+C2)) so downsampled renders read
    ONE native row per output row.  For rasters with E-W terrain features
    (Appalachian aspect map) each native row is a solid colour → stripes.
  * Fix: when rendering at coarser resolution than native (downsampling),
    set the window to NATIVE NS resolution, read every native row in the
    intersection, and area-average (accumulate + divide) native rows into
    output rows.  Viewport EW resolution is kept so GRASS still compresses
    columns.  For upsampling the old nearest-neighbour path is kept (correct).
  * Render window rows/cols are now set to 0 and let G_adjust_Cell_head
    recompute them from the chosen ns_res/ew_res (cleaner than pre-setting).

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Sun, 18 May 2026 20:00:00 +0000

qtgrass (0.3.4) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix workspace viewport: loadWorkspace() now uses the saved display
    extent from the .gxw file (west,south,east,north stored in the
    <display extent="..."> attribute) instead of calling zoomToFullExtent()
    which was dominated by the largest raster and made small-extent layers
    (e.g. elev_lid792_1m covering 700m) appear as a tiny dot.
  * Fallback order: (1) saved .gxw extent, (2) GRASS computational region
    (WIND), (3) zoomToFullExtent() as last resort.
  * Override applied via QTimer::singleShot(50ms) so it runs after the
    per-layer addLayer() auto-zoom for mLayers.size()==1.

